    record rate sqrt(G rho) (canonical) = 0.2847 H0  -> overdamped (asymptotically)
    record rate a0/c (alt)             = 0.1720 H0  -> overdamped (asymptotically)
    record rate sqrt(G rho) (alt)      = 0.3440 H0  -> overdamped (asymptotically)
  [PASS] D1 every record rate lies below (3/2) H_Lambda  max 0.8248 < 1.2371

D2  matter era (EdS, H = 2/(3t)): phi'' + (2/t) phi' + m^2 phi = 0
  [PASS] D2 sin(mt)/(mt) solves the EdS equation (zeros at m t = k pi)  residual 0
  [PASS] D2 u = a^(3/2) phi obeys u'' + (m^2 - 9H^2/4 - 3H'/2) u = 0  
  [PASS] D2 in EdS the effective frequency^2 is exactly m^2 (oscillation for any m once m t > pi)  m**2
    So in the matter era the field turns over once m t ~ pi; in the Lambda era the frequency^2 m^2 - 9H_L^2/4 < 0 for m < 1.5 H_L.

D3  WKB (two timescales): phi = A(t) cos(mt) + B(t) sin(mt), A, B slow
  [PASS] D3 A' = -(3/2) H A and B' = -(3/2) H B  =>  amplitude ∝ a^(-3/2), rho_osc ∝ a^(-3)  A' = -3*H*A(t)/2, B' = -3*H*B(t)/2
    In a0 (small r = rho_osc/V0): relative wave amplitude ∝ rho_osc ∝ (1+z)^3 in the -p and V readings: beta = 3.

D4  readings for a real field phi = A cos(theta), theta' = m (WKB), V = V0 + m^2 phi^2 / 2
